Compile new kernel
    1. Mount "boot" partition:

        $ mount "/boot/"

    2. Clean up old Kernel files in directories "/boot/", "/lib/modules/" and "/usr/src/":

        2.1. List removable Kernel files:

            $ eclean-kernel \
                --pretend \
                --num="2" \
                --sort-order="version" \
                --bootloader="grub2" \
                --no-bootloader-update \
                --no-kernel-install

        2.2. Clean up Kernel files:

            $ eclean-kernel \
                --ask \
                --num="2" \
                --sort-order="version" \
                --bootloader="grub2" \
                --no-bootloader-update \
                --no-kernel-install

    3. Create a new symlink to the newest kernel source directory; not necessary, if use flag "symlink" is set:

        $ cd "/usr/src/"
        $ ln --symbolic --force "linux-<new_kernel_version>-gentoo/" "linux"

    4. Copy the configuration file ".config" from the old kernel source directory to the newer one:

        $ cp "linux-<old_kernel_version>/.config" "linux/.config"

        4.1. Alternative method: Copy the configuration file at runtime:

            $ zcat "/proc/config.gz" > "linux/.config"

    5. Compile the new kernel

        5.1. Adapt new kernel settings and automatically adapt their default values:

            $ cd "/usr/src/linux/"
            $ make olddefconfig

            5.1.1. Alternative method: Adapt new kernel settings manually step-by-step:

                $ make oldconfig

        5.2. Compile the kernel and kernel modules:

            $ available_processors=$(nproc --all)
            $ system_max_load=$(printf "%.2f" "$(( available_processors * 0.80 ))")
            $ make \
                --jobs="${available_processors}" \
                --load-average="${system_max_load}" && \
              make \
                --jobs="${available_processors}" \
                --load-average="${system_max_load}" \
                modules_install

        5.3. Install the kernel and copy it to "/boot/":

            $ make install

    6. Update the initramfs for the new kernel:

        $ update_initramfs

        6.1. Copy the initramfs file to "/boot/":

            $ cp "/usr/src/custom-initramfs-<new_kernel_version>.cpio.gz" "/boot/"

    7. Update the Intel CPU microcode, if necessary:

        $ emerge --oneshot sys-firmware/intel-microcode
        $ iucode_tool \
            --verbose \
            --scan-system="exact" \
            --overwrite \
            --normal-earlyfw \
            --write-earlyfw="/boot/early_ucode.cpio" \
            "/lib/firmware/intel-ucode/"

        7.1. Check the current microcode revision and date:

            $ dmesg | grep "microcode"

        7.2. Reload the microcode manually at runtime (dangerous!):

            $ echo "1" > "/sys/devices/system/cpu/microcode/reload"

        7.3. Check, if the microcode was updated:

            $ dmesg | grep "microcode"

    8. Update the GRUB configuration:

        $ grub-mkconfig --output="/boot/grub/grub.cfg"

    9. Compile all modules for the new kernel:

        $ emerge @module-rebuild

   10. Use the new kernel

        $ reboot

Legend:
- "Clean up" - Keep last three versions. Third version is newest.
